body:
The problem is that YBUpdateActiveSubTransaction, which propagates the subtransaction id to the YB side flushes the pending buffered operations, and when one of the operations throws an exception, it long-jumps to the PG_CATCH block in exec_stmt_block for PL/pgSQL exception handling without doing rest of the transaction state transition stuff.

A potential fix is to flush or reset at the beginning of high-level transaction control functions instead of lower level ones such as Push/PopTransaction. i.e.: flush in ReleaseCurrentSubTransaction, reset in RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction. We need to flush at the beginning of BeginInternalSubTransaction, too, so the statements before entering the blocks are executed in the correct subtransaction context.

exec_stmt_block wraps the exec_stmts call with a PG_TRY block when it has the EXCEPTION clause:
```
 ...
 if (block->exceptions)
 {
    BeginInternalSubTransaction();
    PG_TRY();
    {
      ...
      estate->err_text = NULL;

      rc = exec_stmts(estate, block->body);
      // success if reach here

	  // ... copy return values if any

      ReleaseCurrentSubTransaction();      // commit and clean up
      ...
    }
    PG_CATCH();
    {
      // error if reach here
      RollbackAndReleaseCurrentSubTransaction(); // rollback and clean up

      // handle the exception
    }
    PG_END_TRY();
    ...
  }
  else
  {
    /*
     * Just execute the statements in the block's body
     */
    estate->err_text = NULL;

    rc = exec_stmts(estate, block->body);
  }
```

issue.body:
Consider this example:
```
create table t(k int primary key, v int);
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE test() AS $$
BEGIN
  BEGIN
    insert into t values(100,100);
    insert into t values(100,100);
  EXCEPTION WHEN unique_violation THEN
    insert into t values(200,200);
  END;
  insert into t values(300,300);
END;
$$ LANGUAGE 'plpgsql';
call test();
select * from t;
```
With psql the call succeeds:
```
test=# call test();
CALL
test=# select * from t;
  k  |  v  
-----+-----
 200 | 200
 300 | 300
```
With ysqlsh I get:
```
yugabyte=# call test();
ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint "t_pkey"
yugabyte=# select * from t;
 k | v 
---+---
(0 rows)
```

Note that this is just one example of a case where our batching fails in a procedure, and there may be other scenarios as well.
